  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1221 PM CST Mon Nov 9 2026\n\nMostly clear skies across the area this afternoon as a cold\nfront moves across eastern Nebraska. This is shifting winds \nfrom southerly to northerly this afternoon, with cooler \ntemperatures expected tomorrow. The upper-level pattern shows a \nbroad ridge over the western 2/3rds of the CONUS keeping our \nweather quiet.\n\nA cooler air mass moves in behind the cold front for tomorrow\nwith partly cloudy skies and temperatures forecast to peak in\nthe low-to-mid 60s Tuesday afternoon. Going into midweek we see\nthe ridge flatten with the approach of an upper-level trough\nforecast to move through Tuesday night into early Wednesday. \nWhile this system does pull moisture north, most of it doesn't \narrive until the system is closer to central Iowa. This keeps \nthe best chances for any rain east of our area.\n\nOn the back side of this trough we see a stronger push of\nnortherly winds Wednesday morning leading to breezy conditions\nmuch of the day. This will cap highs in the mid-to-upper 50s\nWednesday afternoon. The cooling trend continues into Thursday\nand Friday as we see that trough continue to deepen to our east\nwith a strong jet streak building southward out of the northern\nRockies into the Missouri & Mississippi River Valleys. While we\nwill be on the western fringe of the much cooler air, we will\nsee highs only peak in the mid 40s Thursday and Friday. Calmer\nwinds Friday morning could lead us to another night where\ntemperatures drop to around or just below freezing. This will be\nour next chance for Omaha to hit freezing. If we do hit freezing\nor lower Friday morning, that would tie our record latest freeze\nat Omaha set back in 2015. \n\nFor those who don't like the cold, temperatures do rebound over\nthe weekend as a transient strongly amplified ridge moves\nthrough Saturday and Sunday. Saturday afternoon could be breezy\nwith winds out of the south, bringing highs back up into the\n50s, especially across our western counties. Sunday we see highs\nwarm back into the mid 50s to near 60 degrees. Milder \nconditions look to continue into next week with a more amplified\nflow regime leading to increasing chances for rain toward \nmidweek.",
